WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.620 --> 00:00:03.889
Hello, everybody.

00:00:03.889 --> 00:00:05.640
Welcome to the webinar today.

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:07.440
My name is Peter Cooper.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.290
This is a short webinar.

00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:11.270
We may not have time to address questions.

00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:15.490
If you think of any, please type them in the
question pod.

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:21.520
We will answer them in a document and post
on the webinar and courses page after the

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:22.520
webinar.

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:28.800
There is a link to the directory on the FTP
site that has these slides, as well as the

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:34.450
other materials that I will be talking about
today.

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:39.239
Also the recording will be in there once we
process it, as well.

00:00:39.239 --> 00:00:41.960
Today we will talk about downloading genome
data.

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:47.329
In particular, I will talk about sequences
and the corresponding annotation files from

00:00:47.329 --> 00:00:49.370
the NCBI FTP site.

00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:51.309
My email address is on the slide; peter.cooper@nih.gov.

00:00:51.309 --> 00:00:54.659
If you have any questions about the content
feel free to write to me.

00:00:54.659 --> 00:00:58.000
We will talk about more content at the end.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:03.190
Today I will talk about these topics.

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:04.190
Quickly with some slides.

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:08.890
Then we will go over to the command line and
show you some, we will use both web pages

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:11.780
in the command line to download genome sequences.

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:18.310
We will talk about Assembly, which is the
main way of organizing the genome sequence

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:19.310
data.

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:23.270
I will talk about what I will call the new
genome FTP site.

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:27.170
It's been there a couple of years but a lot
of people don't know about it.

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:30.180
We will go over some recommendations for downloading
assemblies.

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:31.920
We will show you how to do it.

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:37.140
I will mention the Genomes announce email
list which has been restarted recently.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:40.730
It will be a good way to find out about things
changing about the genome site.

00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:46.120
And like I said we will do some quick demos.

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:51.500
Just a terminology thing, today when I am
talking about genome sequences I will be calling

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:52.500
them assemblies.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:56.810
Those are the things we will be downloading,
the sequences that are models of a genome.

00:01:56.810 --> 00:02:00.800
The genome itself is an abstract concept in
a way.

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:05.470
It's an idealized concept in that it's something
that exists in an individual person or organism.

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:09.670
What we will download today are assemblies
of genomes or representations of genomes.

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:14.269
That's why the resource we will be using is
called Assembly.

00:02:14.269 --> 00:02:16.560
This is what the web resource looks like.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:19.849
It's an Entrez database that we call them.

00:02:19.849 --> 00:02:26.540
It is the way basically of finding out about
what sequences are available, the genome sequence,

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:28.280
for a particular organisms.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:30.409
And even meta genomes.

00:02:30.409 --> 00:02:32.730
It tells you about the assembly structure.

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:37.659
we now have accession numbers for assemblies,
GCA for our GenBank assemblies and GCF for

00:02:37.659 --> 00:02:39.069
the RefSeqs.

00:02:39.069 --> 00:02:43.780
This is the way to track the relationshp between
GEnBank and RefSeq, and it's the basis for

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:47.670
the FTP site, which we will talk about extensively.

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:53.370
Notice that I have circled on the page, this
picture of a page really, this download to

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:54.370
the genome FAQ.

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:59.580
A lot of what I will tell you today is based
on the FAQ.

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:03.290
I did not write it, Paul Kitts did, but it
is very useful.

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:06.840
To get you started on how to do these things
at NCBI.

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:09.049
If we look at a page.

00:03:09.049 --> 00:03:14.860
This is the one for the human assembly, the
name is GRCh38 patch eight.

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:18.540
You can download either the GenBank or RefSeq
versions by following these links.

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:25.069
I will not go over the contents of the Assembly
page because we will mainly talk about the

00:03:25.069 --> 00:03:28.200
FTP site and how to get the data out of there.

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:36.209
If you go to the FTP site, and the base URL
is ftp.ncbinlm.nih.gov, and I will show you

00:03:36.209 --> 00:03:38.849
that in a minute.

00:03:38.849 --> 00:03:40.020
There are three main directories.

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:46.810
There is a genomes/all/ directory and that's
where we will point a lot of our URL paths.

00:03:46.810 --> 00:03:50.769
You cannot browse this currently, if you try
to open it up in your web browser it would

00:03:50.769 --> 00:03:52.489
fall over.

00:03:52.489 --> 00:03:55.129
There are more than 45,000 directories in
there.

00:03:55.129 --> 00:04:01.519
I will talk about the fact that we will restructure
that soon and it will be easier to deal with.

00:04:01.519 --> 00:04:07.579
If you are targeting specific files and directories
and you have a path, then there is no problem.

00:04:07.579 --> 00:04:09.430
You don't want to open it in your web browser.

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:15.769
The two directories that are browsable are
the genomes/genbank and the genomes/refseq.

00:04:15.769 --> 00:04:20.900
They support browsing and downloading for
higher level taxonomic groups, or you can

00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:26.510
drill your way down to the species level and
we will do a little of that today.

00:04:26.510 --> 00:04:31.160
If you go into the FTP directories here, the
genomes/genbank directory on the left and

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:34.080
the genomes/refseq on the right.

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:39.389
You can see the organization by taxonomic
groups; organisms really, not necessarily

00:04:39.389 --> 00:04:40.590
taxonomic groups.

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:44.990
You'll then see the assembly summary text
files.

00:04:44.990 --> 00:04:49.610
These are the files you would go to get the
paths to download data.

00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:54.080
You can certainly see examples of that on
that genomes download FAQ.

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:59.900
Today I will show you how to get to them using
EDirect, which is basically the same thing.

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:04.840
In fact the assembly summary file is one of
the most useful things.

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:06.139
There is this column 20 thing.

00:05:06.139 --> 00:05:07.770
And that has the FTP path.

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:12.650
You can also get that using EDirect.

00:05:12.650 --> 00:05:18.690
If you look at the directory for a particular
assembly, you can see them like this.

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:24.590
On the left is a prokaryotic assembly that
is for the staph aureus reference genome.

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:27.039
The one on the right is for the human reference
genome.

00:05:27.039 --> 00:05:29.229
There are lots of different files in there.

00:05:29.229 --> 00:05:35.050
They are sequence files; there are annotation
files which include things like the gff file.

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:38.240
I will not go over what the files are today.

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:40.669
It's not the point of this particular discussion.

00:05:40.669 --> 00:05:47.680
But the descriptions are included in that
genomes download FAQ.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:53.810
There is also a Readme on the FTP site which
describes the content.

00:05:53.810 --> 00:06:00.150
I wanted to mention to you that - - I mentioned
this in passing a minute ago.

00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:05.360
We will be changing the way the structure
of the genomes/all directory looks.

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:09.680
In principle, the things I am showing you
today will not be affected by the change at

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:10.680
all.

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:16.629
Basically what you will be doing is getting
the path to the directory and they will simply

00:06:16.629 --> 00:06:18.159
change, but you will still be getting the
path.

00:06:18.159 --> 00:06:19.569
Doesn't really matter.

00:06:19.569 --> 00:06:24.681
Just to show you what will happen is we will
make these hierarchy directories so you can

00:06:24.681 --> 00:06:25.830
browse them.

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:30.319
And the directory structure will be based
on the assembly accession number.

00:06:30.319 --> 00:06:36.090
And every three digits will represent another
level in that directory tree to make it possible

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:37.090
to browse it.

00:06:37.090 --> 00:06:42.169
As I said it will not affect the workflows
we will be talking about today.

00:06:42.169 --> 00:06:48.780
You can still browse the genomes/genbannk
or the genomes/refseq directories and you

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:56.180
can use the text files and EDirect to get
the new URLs.

00:06:56.180 --> 00:07:00.169
Notice that the directories are named by the
accession dot version of the assembly and

00:07:00.169 --> 00:07:01.270
the assembly name.

00:07:01.270 --> 00:07:04.100
That will not change either.

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:07.330
So how do you get data out of the FTP site?

00:07:07.330 --> 00:07:15.120
For a few assemblies, a small number, you
can use Entrez.

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:21.560
For batches you can use E direct or the assembly
summary text files.

00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:31.910
You can download directories from the genomes/genbank
or the genomes/refseq directories on the FTP

00:07:31.910 --> 00:07:32.910
site.

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:34.710
I'll show you how to do that.

00:07:34.710 --> 00:07:40.590
I will show you a couple of commandline programs
which are pretty good ways of doing this on

00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:42.280
a Unix system.

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:46.969
You can use wget on Windows as well.

00:07:46.969 --> 00:07:50.169
One way to get things down is to use the NCBI
rsync server.

00:07:50.169 --> 00:07:52.969
It's probably one of the fastest ways of doing
this.

00:07:52.969 --> 00:07:55.539
I will demonstrate that today.

00:07:55.539 --> 00:08:01.599
You could also use wget with these ftp paths,
or curl if you want to, to download the files

00:08:01.599 --> 00:08:02.680
in the directory.

00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:06.819
If you have to download a large number of
files you can write a simple script that calls

00:08:06.819 --> 00:08:10.110
rsync or wget to do this.

00:08:10.110 --> 00:08:15.280
There is a sample Perl script in the materials
directory for this particular talk, and you

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:19.310
can see that if you go to that directory on
the FTP site.

00:08:19.310 --> 00:08:25.210
The code for that Perl script is included
on the file.

00:08:25.210 --> 00:08:30.080
Lets go to the command line and do a few things.

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:36.810
Let's work with getting fungal assemblies,
and I'll show you how to use rsync to download

00:08:36.810 --> 00:08:41.500
data and show you how to use wget to download
directories and files.

00:08:41.500 --> 00:08:48.300
I will go out of this and I will pull up a
command prompt.

00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:52.450
You should be seeing a command prompt here
on one of the servers at NCBI.

00:08:52.450 --> 00:08:59.670
What I will do is on the FTP site there is
a list of commands that we will be going through

00:08:59.670 --> 00:09:01.380
which is basically the demo.

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:06.320
One of the first things I want to do is use
the assembly resource to get a particular

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:11.720
path to a genome.

00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:13.050
This is an Entrez query.

00:09:13.050 --> 00:09:19.820
You can write this yourself or get it by using
the filter options on the assembly resource.

00:09:19.820 --> 00:09:35.560
I will go into another tab over here and go
to the NCBI homepage, go to Assembly.

00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:38.040
I have some filters on and I will turn them
off.

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:43.770
This is the assembly homepage.

00:09:43.770 --> 00:09:47.639
This by the way at the bottom is the link
to the FAQ which will be important if you

00:09:47.639 --> 00:09:49.290
want to do these things.

00:09:49.290 --> 00:09:57.380
I will go ahead and paste this query in.

00:09:57.380 --> 00:10:01.160
So I have six of the complete fungal genomes
here.

00:10:01.160 --> 00:10:02.160
You can see them.

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:07.089
What I want to do is get you to the path to
download one of these genomes.

00:10:07.089 --> 00:10:08.240
This is the one we will download.

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:14.160
This is the Saccharomyces cerevisae, the yeast
reference genome.

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:21.790
If I click here it takes me to the assembly
record.

00:10:21.790 --> 00:10:24.150
There are lots of things here we don't have
time to talk about.

00:10:24.150 --> 00:10:27.420
Notice you can see the assembly accession
number for the RefSeq.

00:10:27.420 --> 00:10:31.470
You also can see the GenBank referenced here.

00:10:31.470 --> 00:10:40.780
We can go to the RefSeq assembly page here
to download this, and we can see the URL that

00:10:40.780 --> 00:10:46.240
goes into the genome/all a specific directory
for the assembly accession and then the the

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:48.930
underscore character.

00:10:48.930 --> 00:10:53.910
So with an ftp client or even a web browser,
you can download files individually, but let

00:10:53.910 --> 00:10:57.260
me show you that you can do this easily using
rsync.

00:10:57.260 --> 00:11:00.800
I will show you command lines for a few minutes.

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:03.640
I will not explain everything.

00:11:03.640 --> 00:11:08.079
If you want to know what the options are you
can look at the man pages for the different

00:11:08.079 --> 00:11:16.010
programs, the man page for rsync and the man
page for wget when we get there.

00:11:16.010 --> 00:11:20.360
Let's do a rsync command to download all of
this data that's in there.

00:11:20.360 --> 00:11:25.269
That will be the sequence file and annotation
files plus, this directory here, which contains

00:11:25.269 --> 00:11:30.589
more files, which are chromosome by chromosome
and information about how the assembly was

00:11:30.589 --> 00:11:32.209
put together.

00:11:32.209 --> 00:11:37.150
I am going to go back to my script here.

00:11:37.150 --> 00:11:51.690
This is the rsync command we will do to get
this down.

00:11:51.690 --> 00:11:56.890
I pulled the trigger pretty fast on that one,
so let me go back up here because I wanted

00:11:56.890 --> 00:12:01.079
to mention a couple things.

00:12:01.079 --> 00:12:03.220
This is the basic command.

00:12:03.220 --> 00:12:09.000
These things have to do with whether we are
going to keep symbolic links and turn them

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:10.000
into the files.

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:14.000
Recursive means we will go down the directory
tree structure.

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:20.850
This is essentially the URL to the FTP site,
the protocols is replaced with rsync.

00:12:20.850 --> 00:12:22.649
That's what you do.

00:12:22.649 --> 00:12:26.120
And then I just told it to put this in a directory
called yeast.

00:12:26.120 --> 00:12:29.420
And it tells me the information from the server.

00:12:29.420 --> 00:12:32.709
It downloaded all of that stuff.

00:12:32.709 --> 00:12:38.690
Including the directory structure to my hard
drive.

00:12:38.690 --> 00:12:39.690
And there is the yeast directory.

00:12:39.690 --> 00:12:56.440
And there are all the files that were present
on the site.

00:12:56.440 --> 00:12:59.510
While we are here let me show you something
else.

00:12:59.510 --> 00:13:03.380
Another kind of download that we might want
to do.

00:13:03.380 --> 00:13:10.149
so we're in genomes/all, but what if we want
to get all of the fungi reference sequences.

00:13:10.149 --> 00:13:20.199
What I can do is change this genomes/all directory
and I will do this by hand.

00:13:20.199 --> 00:13:26.490
Now I am looking at the genomes/refseq directory
which contains the different organism groups.

00:13:26.490 --> 00:13:32.720
I can download for example suppose I want
to download all of the fungal genomes here.

00:13:32.720 --> 00:13:35.350
Or all of them in fact if I wanted to.

00:13:35.350 --> 00:13:45.930
I could write a rsync command to do that easily.

00:13:45.930 --> 00:13:51.089
This is the one I want to use and we will
discuss this one a little bit.

00:13:51.089 --> 00:13:57.480
What this one will do is it will go through
here and avoid a lot of the directories in

00:13:57.480 --> 00:13:58.480
there.

00:13:58.480 --> 00:14:02.120
Basically it will get rid of the assembly
structure directory and get rid of the latest

00:14:02.120 --> 00:14:03.790
assembly versions.

00:14:03.790 --> 00:14:07.830
And it will get me the protein FASTA file
from each one of these.

00:14:07.830 --> 00:14:11.449
This is a rather long complex rsync command
line.

00:14:11.449 --> 00:14:15.199
You look at the options yourself and you can
see what they do.

00:14:15.199 --> 00:14:16.399
Let's try to run this one.

00:14:16.399 --> 00:14:25.380
What it will do for us is it will give us
all of the protein files.

00:14:25.380 --> 00:14:38.160
This one will take about 15 seconds or so.

00:14:38.160 --> 00:14:48.250
If you notice what happens is it's reproducing
the directory structure tree that is present

00:14:48.250 --> 00:14:49.250
in NCBI.

00:14:49.250 --> 00:14:53.710
This is basically mirroring the data, but
it is getting a specific file from each directory.

00:14:53.710 --> 00:15:11.279
We will get the protein files from each one
of these fungal genomes.

00:15:11.279 --> 00:15:13.389
We can see they are there.

00:15:13.389 --> 00:15:17.949
If we want to we can go into the saccchromyces
directory.

00:15:17.949 --> 00:15:29.240
So we have the reference genome directory,
which is retained from the NCBI side.

00:15:29.240 --> 00:15:30.540
Also this one.

00:15:30.540 --> 00:15:34.129
This is the assembly accession.

00:15:34.129 --> 00:15:37.839
You can do some things to make it so you did
not get all of these directories if you did

00:15:37.839 --> 00:15:42.139
not want them.

00:15:42.139 --> 00:15:44.100
There is the protein file.

00:15:44.100 --> 00:15:56.440
Those are the files of all the proteins that
were annotated on that particular genome.

00:15:56.440 --> 00:16:00.980
Now there are some other examples in here
using rsync and you can take a look at them.

00:16:00.980 --> 00:16:05.770
I want to move onto the next way of doing
things, which is to try to use wget, and you

00:16:05.770 --> 00:16:08.730
can also use rsync to do the things as well.

00:16:08.730 --> 00:16:14.089
This is a strategy of trying to get the FTP
paths.

00:16:14.089 --> 00:16:18.560
Either from the assembly_summary.txt file
or to get them from EDirect.

00:16:18.560 --> 00:16:24.440
I will use EDirect and we have a webinar on
that if you need more information.

00:16:24.440 --> 00:16:34.120
I will use that to get the paths.

00:16:34.120 --> 00:16:40.589
This is my EDirect command and it will use
the same query we used in the assembly resource.

00:16:40.589 --> 00:16:47.690
Then I will use efetch to get the document
summary, which has a section that has the

00:16:47.690 --> 00:16:50.269
FTP file paths in it.

00:16:50.269 --> 00:16:55.660
I will get out the RefSeq FTP directories
out of that.

00:16:55.660 --> 00:17:02.020
Let's try that and see how it works.

00:17:02.020 --> 00:17:08.459
Only thing I have added to this is there is
a little sed command at the end that will

00:17:08.459 --> 00:17:12.939
add that forward slash directory symbol to
make them directories, which will make them

00:17:12.939 --> 00:17:22.400
work better with wget.

00:17:22.400 --> 00:17:29.510
That file contains URLs that would take me
directly to those directories and I can potentially

00:17:29.510 --> 00:17:32.539
download those exactly the same way I did
before.

00:17:32.539 --> 00:17:35.610
I could use a different way to do that if
I want to.

00:17:35.610 --> 00:17:41.470
And the way I was going to show you was to
use wget.

00:17:41.470 --> 00:17:45.700
What this will do is give you the same kind
of information I got for the yeast for all

00:17:45.700 --> 00:17:55.360
of those complete genomes.

00:17:55.360 --> 00:17:59.330
This is a taller order, maybe not, than the
one we did a minute ago.

00:17:59.330 --> 00:18:05.270
And the different options here are ways of
dealing with the kinds of default directors

00:18:05.270 --> 00:18:07.610
that wget will put on your machine.

00:18:07.610 --> 00:18:12.010
You can organize those in different ways by
choosing how many directories cut, and so

00:18:12.010 --> 00:18:13.090
on and so forth.

00:18:13.090 --> 00:18:17.169
Again I refer you to the wget man page.

00:18:17.169 --> 00:18:33.760
I will put it into a directory called complete
fungal.

00:18:33.760 --> 00:18:39.659
So I now I have directories for all of the
different, the six complete genomes we saw

00:18:39.659 --> 00:18:47.400
a minute ago using the assembly resource.

00:18:47.400 --> 00:18:50.450
For example I went into the yeast directory
here.

00:18:50.450 --> 00:19:01.360
What I did, one of the switches I added - - I
did not get the semi-redundant, the assembly_structure

00:19:01.360 --> 00:19:03.480
directory, I did not get that.

00:19:03.480 --> 00:19:06.550
If I wanted to I could've done it by changing
the level.

00:19:06.550 --> 00:19:10.669
I have a level one here which means I won't
descend, but I could've descended into the

00:19:10.669 --> 00:19:11.669
next level.

00:19:11.669 --> 00:19:17.680
The last example which I will not do today
goes ahead and gets a particular file out

00:19:17.680 --> 00:19:19.440
of each of these directories.

00:19:19.440 --> 00:19:21.210
You can do that with wget.

00:19:21.210 --> 00:19:25.630
You can also do that with the Perl script
included in the directory for the materials

00:19:25.630 --> 00:19:30.440
for this particular FTP site.

00:19:30.440 --> 00:19:35.460
All of that stuff including additional examples
are in that text file on the FTP site.

